AMC.add({"comp": "amc12", "id": "2021SpringB", "label": "2021 Spring B", "problems": [{"q": "<p> How many integer values satisfy <span class=\"tex\">|x|&lt;3 \\pi?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">9</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">10</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">18</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">19</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">20</span>"], "a": "D", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">9</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">10</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">18</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">19</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">20</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p><strong>Answer (D):</strong> Because <span class=\"tex\">9&lt;3 \\pi&lt;10</span>, the <span class=\"tex\">19</span> consecutive integers from <span class=\"tex\">-9</span> to <span class=\"tex\">9</span> satisfy the inequality.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2021-spring/part-b/problem-1"}, {"q": "<p> At a math contest, <span class=\"tex\">57</span> students are wearing blue shirts, and another <span class=\"tex\">75</span> students are wearing yellow shirts. The <span class=\"tex\">132</span> students are assigned into <span class=\"tex\">66</span> points. In exactly <span class=\"tex\">23</span> of these pairs, both students are wearing blue shirts. In how many pairs are both students wearing yellow shirts?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">23</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">32</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">37</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">41</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">64</span>"], "a": "B", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">23</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">32</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">37</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">41</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">64</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p><strong>Answer (B):</strong> There are 46 blue-shirted students in the all-blue pairs. Thus <span class=\"tex\">57-46=11</span> blue-shirted students are paired with yellow-shirted students, leaving <span class=\"tex\">75-11=64</span> yellow-shirted students in all-yellow pairs. Hence there are <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{64}{2}=32</span> all-yellow pairs.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2021-spring/part-b/problem-2"}, {"q": "<p> Suppose</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">2+\\dfrac{1}{1+\\dfrac{1}{2+\\dfrac{2}{3+x}}}=\\dfrac{144}{53}</span></p><p>What is the value of <span class=\"tex\">x?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{3}{4}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{7}{8}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{14}{15}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{37}{38}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{52}{53}</span>"], "a": "A", "s": "<p><strong>Answer (A):</strong> Subtracting 2 from both sides and taking reciprocals gives</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">1+\\frac{1}{2+\\frac{2}{3+x}}=\\frac{53}{38} .</span></p><p>Subtracting 1 from both sides and taking reciprocals gives</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">2+\\frac{2}{3+x}=\\frac{38}{15} .</span></p><p>Subtracting 2 from both sides and taking reciprocals gives</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\frac{3+x}{2}=\\frac{15}{8} .</span></p><p>Thus <span class=\"tex\">x=2 \\cdot \\frac{15}{8}-3=\\frac{15}{4}-\\frac{12}{4}=\\frac{3}{4}</span>.</p>\n<p>Note: The number <span class=\"tex\">e</span> can be represented by the following continued fraction.</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">2+\\frac{1}{1+\\frac{1}{2+\\frac{2}{3+\\frac{3}{4+\\frac{4}{5+\\cdots}}}}}</span></p><p>The fraction <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{144}{53} \\approx 2.717</span> is an approximation for <span class=\"tex\">e \\approx 2.718</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2021-spring/part-b/problem-3"}, {"q": "<p> Ms. Blackwell gives an exam to two classes. The mean of the scores of the students in the morning class is <span class=\"tex\">84</span>, and the afternoon class mean score is <span class=\"tex\">70</span>. The ratio of the number of students in the morning class to the number of students in the afternoon class is <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{3}{4}</span>. What is the mean of the scores of all the students?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">74</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">75</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">76</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">77</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">78</span>"], "a": "C", "s": "<p><strong>Answer (C):</strong> Let <span class=\"tex\">n</span> be the number of students in the morning class and <span class=\"tex\">m</span> the number of students in the afternoon class. It is given that <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{n}{m}=\\frac{3}{4}</span>, so <span class=\"tex\">n=\\frac{3}{4} m</span>. The total number of points scored by all <span class=\"tex\">n+m</span> students is <span class=\"tex\">84 n+70 m</span>, so the requested mean is</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\frac{84 n+70 m}{n+m}=\\frac{84\\left(\\frac{3}{4} m\\right)+70 m}{\\frac{3}{4} m+m}=\\frac{133 m}{\\frac{7}{4} m}=76</span></p><p><strong>OR</strong></p>\n<p>The requested value is just the weighted average of 84 and 70 , with weights 3 and 4 :</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\frac{3 \\cdot 84+4 \\cdot 70}{3+4}=\\frac{532}{7}=76.</span></p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2021-spring/part-b/problem-4"}, {"q": "<p> The point <span class=\"tex\">P(a, b)</span> in the <span class=\"tex\">x y</span>-plane is first rotated counterclockwise by <span class=\"tex\">90^{\\circ}</span> around the point <span class=\"tex\">(1,5)</span> and then reflected about the line <span class=\"tex\">y=-x</span>. The image of <span class=\"tex\">P</span> after these two transformations is at <span class=\"tex\">(-6,3)</span>. What is <span class=\"tex\">b-a?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">1</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">3</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">5</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">7</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">9</span>"], "a": "D", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">1</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">3</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">5</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">7</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">9</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p><strong>Answer (D):</strong> The image of <span class=\"tex\">P</span> before the reflection about <span class=\"tex\">y=-x</span> must have been ( <span class=\"tex\">-3,6</span> ). The coordinates of <span class=\"tex\">P</span> can then be found by rotating this point clockwise by <span class=\"tex\">90^{\\circ}</span> around the point <span class=\"tex\">( 1,5 )</span>. Because ( <span class=\"tex\">-3,6</span> ) is 4 units to the left and 1 unit up from <span class=\"tex\">( 1,5 )</span>, the point <span class=\"tex\">P</span> must be 4 units up and 1 unit to the right from <span class=\"tex\">( 1,5 )</span>. Therefore the coordinates of <span class=\"tex\">P</span> must be <span class=\"tex\">(2,9)</span>, so <span class=\"tex\">b-a=9-2=7</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"diagram\"><svg height=\"280px\" style=\"background: transparent; background-color: transparent; color-scheme: light dark;\" width=\"349px\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><defs></defs><g><g><g><g><g><image height=\"280\" width=\"348.05\"/></g></g></g></g></g></svg></div>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2021-spring/part-b/problem-5"}, {"q": "<p> An inverted cone with base radius <span class=\"tex\">12 \\mathrm{~cm}</span> and height <span class=\"tex\">18 \\mathrm{~cm}</span> is full of water. The water is poured into a tall cylinder whose horizontal base has a radius of <span class=\"tex\">24 \\mathrm{~cm}</span>. What is the height in centimeters of the water in the cylinder?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">1.5</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">3</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">4</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">4.5</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">6</span>"], "a": "A", "s": "<p><strong>Answer (A):</strong> Let <span class=\"tex\">h</span> be the height in centimeters of the water in the cylinder. The volume of the water in the cone is <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{1}{3} \\cdot \\pi \\cdot 12^{2} \\cdot 18</span>. The volume of the water in the cylinder is <span class=\"tex\">\\pi \\cdot 24^{2} \\cdot h</span>. The volumes are the same, so <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{1}{3} \\cdot \\pi \\cdot 12^{2} \\cdot 18=\\pi \\cdot 24^{2} \\cdot h</span>. It follows that</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">h=\\frac{\\frac{1}{3} \\cdot \\pi \\cdot 12^{2} \\cdot 18}{\\pi \\cdot 24^{2}}=\\frac{3}{2}=1.5 .</span></p><p><strong>OR</strong></p>\n<p>The volume of a cylinder is 3 times the volume of a cone with the same base radius and height. Doubling the radius of the cone multiplies the volume by <span class=\"tex\">2^{2}=4</span>. Therefore the depth of the water in the cylinder will be <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{1}{3} \\cdot \\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1}{12}</span> the depth of the water in the cone, namely <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{1}{12} \\cdot 18=\\frac{3}{2}=1.5</span> centimeters.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2021-spring/part-b/problem-6"}, {"q": "<p> Let <span class=\"tex\">N=34 \\cdot 34 \\cdot 63 \\cdot 270</span>. What is the ratio of the sum of the odd divisors of <span class=\"tex\">N</span> to the sum of the even divisors of <span class=\"tex\">N?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">1: 16</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">1: 15</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">1: 14</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">1: 8</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">1: 3</span>"], "a": "C", "s": "<p><strong>Answer (C):</strong> Let <span class=\"tex\">k</span> be the sum of the odd divisors of <span class=\"tex\">N</span>. The greatest power of 2 that divides <span class=\"tex\">N</span> is <span class=\"tex\">2^{3}</span>. Odd divisors of <span class=\"tex\">N</span> multiplied by 2,4 , and 8 yield all the even divisors of <span class=\"tex\">N</span>. Therefore the sum of the even divisors of <span class=\"tex\">N</span> is <span class=\"tex\">(2+4+8) k=14 k</span>, and the requested ratio is <span class=\"tex\">1: 14</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2021-spring/part-b/problem-7"}, {"q": "<p> Three equally spaced parallel lines intersect a circle, creating three chords of lengths <span class=\"tex\">38,38</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">34</span>. What is the distance between two adjacent parallel lines?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">5 \\dfrac{1}{2}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">6</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">6 \\dfrac{1}{2}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">7</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">7 \\dfrac{1}{2}</span>"], "a": "B", "s": "<p><strong>Answer (B):</strong> Let the center of the circle be <span class=\"tex\">O</span>, let its radius be <span class=\"tex\">r</span>, let chord <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{A B}</span> have length 34 , and let chords <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{C D}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{E F}</span> each have length 38 . Because <span class=\"tex\">C D=E F</span>, chords <span class=\"tex\">C D</span> and <span class=\"tex\">E F</span> are equidistant from <span class=\"tex\">O</span>. Let <span class=\"tex\">d</span> be the distance from <span class=\"tex\">O</span> to the midpoint of <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{C D}</span>; then the distance between adjacent parallel lines is <span class=\"tex\">2 d</span>. See the figure. Then the Pythagorean Theorem gives</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">d^{2}+19^{2}=r^{2}=(3 d)^{2}+17^{2} .</span></p><p>Thus <span class=\"tex\">8 d^{2}=361-289=72</span>, so <span class=\"tex\">d=3</span>, and the distance between two adjacent parallel lines is <span class=\"tex\">2 d=6</span>. (The circle has radius <span class=\"tex\">r=\\sqrt{3^{2}+19^{2}}=\\sqrt{370} \\approx 19.2</span>.)</p>\n<div class=\"diagram\"><svg height=\"340px\" style=\"background: transparent; background-color: transparent; color-scheme: light dark;\" width=\"396px\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><defs></defs><g><g><g><g><g><image height=\"340\" width=\"395.48\"/></g></g></g></g></g></svg></div>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2021-spring/part-b/problem-8"}, {"q": "<p> What is the value of</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\dfrac{\\log _{2} 80}{\\log _{40} 2}-\\dfrac{\\log _{2} 160}{\\log _{20} 2} ?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">0</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">1</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{5}{4}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">2</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\log _{2} 5</span>"], "a": "D", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">0</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">1</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{5}{4}</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">2</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">\\log _{2} 5</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p><strong>Answer (D):</strong> Recall that <span class=\"tex\">\\log _{a} b</span> is the reciprocal of <span class=\"tex\">\\log _{b} a</span>. Therefore the given expression equals</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{aligned}\n&amp; \\log _{2} 80 \\cdot \\log _{2} 40-\\log _{2} 160 \\cdot \\log _{2} 20 \\\\\n&amp; \\quad=\\log _{2}\\left(2^{4} \\cdot 5\\right) \\cdot \\log _{2}\\left(2^{3} \\cdot 5\\right)-\\log _{2}\\left(2^{5} \\cdot 5\\right) \\cdot \\log _{2}\\left(2^{2} \\cdot 5\\right)\n\\end{aligned}</span></p><p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{aligned}\n&amp; =\\left(4+\\log _{2} 5\\right)\\left(3+\\log _{2} 5\\right)-\\left(5+\\log _{2} 5\\right)\\left(2+\\log _{2} 5\\right) \\\\\n&amp; =12+7 \\log _{2} 5+\\left(\\log _{2} 5\\right)^{2}-10-7 \\log _{2} 5-\\left(\\log _{2} 5\\right)^{2} \\\\\n&amp; =2.\n\\end{aligned}</span></p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2021-spring/part-b/problem-9"}, {"q": "<p> Two distinct numbers are selected from the set <span class=\"tex\">\\{1,2,3,4, \\ldots, 36,37\\}</span> so that the sum of the remaining <span class=\"tex\">35</span> numbers is the product of these two numbers. What is the difference of these two numbers?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">5</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">7</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">8</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">9</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">10</span>"], "a": "E", "s": "<p><strong>Answer (E):</strong> The sum of the first 37 positive integers is <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{37 \\cdot 38}{2}=703</span>. Let <span class=\"tex\">a</span> and <span class=\"tex\">b</span> be the selected numbers. Then <span class=\"tex\">703-a-b=a b</span>. Adding <span class=\"tex\">a+b+1</span> to both sides of the equation and factoring gives <span class=\"tex\">704=(a+1)(b+1)</span>. Because <span class=\"tex\">704=32 \\cdot 22</span>, but no other product of positive integers less than 38 equals 704 , it follows that the two numbers are 31 and 21. The requested difference is 10.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2021-spring/part-b/problem-10"}, {"q": "<p> Triangle <span class=\"tex\">A B C</span> has <span class=\"tex\">A B=13, B C=14</span> and <span class=\"tex\">A C=15</span>. Let <span class=\"tex\">P</span> be the point on <span class=\"tex\">AC</span> such that <span class=\"tex\">P C=10</span>. There are exactly two points <span class=\"tex\">D</span> and <span class=\"tex\">E</span> on line <span class=\"tex\">B P</span> such that quadrilaterals <span class=\"tex\">A B C D</span> and <span class=\"tex\">A B C E</span> are trapezoids. What is the distance <span class=\"tex\">D E?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{42}{5}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">6 \\sqrt{2}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{84}{5}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">12 \\sqrt{2}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">18</span>"], "a": "D", "s": "<p><strong>Answer (D):</strong> Without loss of generality, suppose that <span class=\"tex\">D</span> is closer to <span class=\"tex\">P</span> than is <span class=\"tex\">E</span>, and let <span class=\"tex\">P D=x</span>. Note that <span class=\"tex\">A P=A C-P C=15-10=5</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle A P D \\sim \\triangle C P B</span>; therefore <span class=\"tex\">B P=x \\cdot \\frac{10}{5}=2 x</span>. Because <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle A P B \\sim \\triangle C P E</span>, it follows that <span class=\"tex\">E P=2 B P=4 x</span>. Hence <span class=\"tex\">D E=E P-D P=3 x</span>. The Law of Cosines in <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle A C B</span> gives</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">13^{2}=14^{2}+15^{2}-2 \\cdot 14 \\cdot 15 \\cdot \\cos \\angle A C B,</span></p><p>whence <span class=\"tex\">\\cos \\angle A C B=\\frac{3}{5}</span>. Then the Law of Cosines in <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle P C B</span> gives</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">B P^{2}=10^{2}+14^{2}-2 \\cdot 10 \\cdot 14 \\cdot \\frac{3}{5}=10^{2}+14^{2}-14 \\cdot 12=128 .</span></p><p>Therefore <span class=\"tex\">B P=8 \\sqrt{2}, x=4 \\sqrt{2}</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">D E=3 \\cdot 4 \\sqrt{2}=12 \\sqrt{2}</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"diagram\"><svg height=\"220px\" style=\"background: transparent; background-color: transparent; color-scheme: light dark;\" width=\"446px\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><defs></defs><g><g><g><g><g><image height=\"220\" width=\"445.56\"/></g></g></g></g></g></svg></div>\n<p><strong>OR</strong></p>\n<p>As in the first solution, <span class=\"tex\">B P=2 x</span> and <span class=\"tex\">E P=4 x</span>, so <span class=\"tex\">E B=6 x</span>. Furthermore, <span class=\"tex\">E C=A B \\cdot \\frac{10}{5}=26</span>. Using Stewart's Theorem on <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle C E B</span> with base <span class=\"tex\">E B=6 x</span> and cevian <span class=\"tex\">C P=10</span> gives</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">26^{2}(2 x)+14^{2}(4 x)=(6 x)\\left(10^{2}+4 x \\cdot 2 x\\right) .</span></p><p>Solving this equation gives <span class=\"tex\">x=4 \\sqrt{2}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">D E=3 x=12 \\sqrt{2}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2021-spring/part-b/problem-11"}, {"q": "<p> Suppose that <span class=\"tex\">S</span> is a finite set of positive integers. If the greatest integer in <span class=\"tex\">S</span> is removed from <span class=\"tex\">S</span>, then the average value (arithmetic mean) of the integers remaining is <span class=\"tex\">32</span>. If the least integer is <span class=\"tex\">S</span> is also removed, then the average value of the integers remaining is <span class=\"tex\">35</span>. If the greatest integer is then returned to the set, the average value of the integers rises to <span class=\"tex\">40</span>. The greatest integer in the original set <span class=\"tex\">S</span> is <span class=\"tex\">72</span> greater than the least integer in <span class=\"tex\">S</span>. What is the average value of all the integers in the set <span class=\"tex\">S?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">36.2</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">36.4</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">36.6</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">36.8</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">37</span>"], "a": "D", "s": "<p>Answer (D): Let <span class=\"tex\">n</span> be the number of elements of <span class=\"tex\">S</span>, let <span class=\"tex\">s</span> be the sum of all the elements, let <span class=\"tex\">x</span> be the greatest element, and let <span class=\"tex\">y</span> be the least element. The information given in the problem leads to the following equations:</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{aligned}\ns-x &amp; =32(n-1) \\\\\ns-x-y &amp; =35(n-2) \\\\\ns-y &amp; =40(n-1) \\\\\nx-y &amp; =72\n\\end{aligned}</span></p><p>Subtracting the first equation from the third gives <span class=\"tex\">x-y=8(n-1)</span>, and because <span class=\"tex\">x-y=72</span>, this implies <span class=\"tex\">n-1=9</span>, so <span class=\"tex\">n=10</span>. Thus</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{aligned}\ns-x &amp; =288 \\\\\ns-x-y &amp; =280 \\\\\ns-y &amp; =360 .\n\\end{aligned}</span></p><p>Adding the first and third equations and subtracting the second gives <span class=\"tex\">s=368</span>. Because <span class=\"tex\">n=10</span>, the requested average is <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{368}{10}=36.8</span>. One such set is <span class=\"tex\">\\{8,31,32,33,34,36,37,38,39,80\\}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2021-spring/part-b/problem-12"}, {"q": "<p> How many values of <span class=\"tex\">\\theta</span> in the interval <span class=\"tex\">0&lt;\\theta \\leq 2 \\pi</span> satisfy</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">1-3 \\sin \\theta+5 \\cos 3 \\theta=0 ?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">2</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">4</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">5</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">6</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">8</span>"], "a": "D", "s": "<p><strong>Answer (D):</strong> Rewrite the equation as <span class=\"tex\">3 \\sin \\theta-1=5 \\cos 3 \\theta</span>. One can make a fairly accurate sketch of the functions on the left-hand and right-hand sides of this equation. For <span class=\"tex\">y=3 \\sin x-1</span>, move the normal sine graph with amplitude 3 down 1 unit. The graph of <span class=\"tex\">y=5 \\cos 3 x</span> is the graph of <span class=\"tex\">y=\\cos x</span> stretched vertically and compressed horizontally so as to have amplitude 5 and period <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}</span>. It can be seen that the two graphs intersect 6 times for <span class=\"tex\">0&lt;x \\leq 2 \\pi</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"diagram\"><svg height=\"280px\" style=\"background: transparent; background-color: transparent; color-scheme: light dark;\" width=\"345px\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><defs></defs><g><g><g><g><g><image height=\"280\" width=\"344.97\"/></g></g></g></g></g></svg></div>\n<p><strong>OR</strong></p>\n<p>Let <span class=\"tex\">T(\\theta)=1-3 \\sin \\theta+5 \\cos 3 \\theta</span>. To show that <span class=\"tex\">T(\\theta)</span> must have at least 6 roots, note that <span class=\"tex\">|\\sin \\theta| \\leq 1</span> for all <span class=\"tex\">\\theta</span>, so <span class=\"tex\">|1-3 \\sin \\theta| \\leq 1+|3 \\sin \\theta| \\leq 1+3=4&lt;5</span>. Hence <span class=\"tex\">T(\\theta)&gt;0</span> when <span class=\"tex\">\\cos 3 \\theta=1</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">T(\\theta)&lt;0</span> when <span class=\"tex\">\\cos 3 \\theta=-1</span>. Thus</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{aligned}\nT(0) &amp; &gt;0 \\\\\nT\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right) &amp; &lt;0 \\\\\nT\\left(\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}\\right) &amp; &gt;0 \\\\\nT(\\pi) &amp; &lt;0 \\\\\nT\\left(\\frac{4 \\pi}{3}\\right) &amp; &gt;0 \\\\\nT\\left(\\frac{5 \\pi}{3}\\right) &amp; &lt;0, \\text { and } \\\\\nT(2 \\pi) &amp; &gt;0\n\\end{aligned}</span></p><p>The interval <span class=\"tex\">(0,2 \\pi]</span> can be partitioned into 6 subintervals each of length <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{\\pi}{3}</span> in which <span class=\"tex\">T(\\theta)</span> has a change of sign, and hence a zero.</p>\n<p>To show that <span class=\"tex\">T</span> has no more than 6 roots, write <span class=\"tex\">\\sin \\theta</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\cos 3 \\theta</span> in terms of complex exponentials, so that</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{aligned}\nT(\\theta) &amp; =\\frac{5}{2} e^{-3 i \\theta}-\\frac{3 i}{2} e^{-i \\theta}+1+\\frac{3 i}{2} e^{i \\theta}+\\frac{5}{2} e^{3 i \\theta} \\\\\n&amp; =e^{-3 i \\theta}\\left(\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{3 i}{2} e^{2 i \\theta}+e^{3 i \\theta}+\\frac{3 i}{2} e^{4 i \\theta}+\\frac{5}{2} e^{6 i \\theta}\\right) \\\\\n&amp; =z^{-3}\\left(\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{3 i}{2} z^{2}+z^{3}+\\frac{3 i}{2} z^{4}+\\frac{5}{2} z^{6}\\right)\n\\end{aligned}</span></p><p>Let <span class=\"tex\">P(z)=\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{3 i}{2} z^{2}+z^{3}+\\frac{3 i}{2} z^{4}+\\frac{5}{2} z^{6}</span>. Then <span class=\"tex\">T(\\theta)=z^{-3} P(z)</span>. Distinct <span class=\"tex\">\\theta</span> in the interval <span class=\"tex\">0&lt;\\theta \\leq 2 \\pi</span> correspond to distinct <span class=\"tex\">z</span> on the unit circle <span class=\"tex\">|z|=1</span> in the complex plane. Because a polynomial can have no more roots than its degree, <span class=\"tex\">P</span> has at most 6 roots, and hence <span class=\"tex\">T</span> has at most 6 roots in a period. More generally, if <span class=\"tex\">T(\\theta)</span> is a trigonometric polynomial of degree <span class=\"tex\">N</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">T</span> is not identically 0 , then it has at most <span class=\"tex\">2 N</span> roots, counting multiplicity, because the roots of <span class=\"tex\">T(\\theta)</span> correspond to the roots on the unit circle of an algebraic polynomial <span class=\"tex\">P(z)</span> of degree <span class=\"tex\">2 N</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2021-spring/part-b/problem-13"}, {"q": "<p> Let <span class=\"tex\">A B C D</span> be a rectangle and let <span class=\"tex\">DM</span> be a segment perpendicular to the plane of <span class=\"tex\">A B C D</span>. Suppose that <span class=\"tex\">DM</span> has integer length, and the lengths of <span class=\"tex\">MA,MC</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">MB</span> are consecutive odd positive integers (in this order). What is the volume of pyramid <span class=\"tex\">M A B C D?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">24 \\sqrt{5}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">60</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">28 \\sqrt{5}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">66</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">8 \\sqrt{70}</span>"], "a": "A", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">24 \\sqrt{5}</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">60</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">28 \\sqrt{5}</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">66</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">8 \\sqrt{70}</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p><strong>Answer (A):</strong> Let <span class=\"tex\">A D=B C=a, A B=C D=b</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">D M=h</span>, where <span class=\"tex\">h</span> is a positive integer. Then <span class=\"tex\">M A=\\sqrt{h^{2}+a^{2}}, M C=\\sqrt{h^{2}+b^{2}}</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">M B=\\sqrt{h^{2}+a^{2}+b^{2}}</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"diagram\"><svg height=\"310px\" style=\"background: transparent; background-color: transparent; color-scheme: light dark;\" width=\"316px\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><defs></defs><g><g><g><g><g><image height=\"310\" width=\"315.89\"/></g></g></g></g></g></svg></div>\n<p>Because <span class=\"tex\">M A, M C</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">M B</span> are consecutive odd positive integers, there exists a nonnegative integer <span class=\"tex\">k</span> such that <span class=\"tex\">M A=2 k+1, M C=2 k+3</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">M B=2 k+5</span>. It follows that</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{aligned}\n&amp; (2 k+1)^{2}=h^{2}+a^{2} \\\\\n&amp; (2 k+3)^{2}=h^{2}+b^{2}, \\text { and } \\\\\n&amp; (2 k+5)^{2}=h^{2}+a^{2}+b^{2} .\n\\end{aligned}</span></p><p>Adding the first two equations above yields</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">(2 k+1)^{2}+(2 k+3)^{2}=2 h^{2}+a^{2}+b^{2} .</span></p><p>Subtracting the third equation above from the previous equation gives</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">(2 k+1)^{2}+(2 k+3)^{2}-(2 k+5)^{2}=h^{2} .</span></p><p>This simplifies to</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">4 k^{2}-4 k=15+h^{2} .</span></p><p>Completing the square on the left-hand side gives <span class=\"tex\">(2 k-1)^{2}=16+h^{2}</span>, so <span class=\"tex\">(2 k-1)^{2}-h^{2}=16</span>. Factoring the left-hand side of this equation as a difference of squares gives</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">(2 k-1-h)(2 k-1+h)=16 .</span></p><p>Because <span class=\"tex\">2 k-1-h</span> and <span class=\"tex\">2 k-1+h</span> have the same parity (so neither factor can equal 1 ) and <span class=\"tex\">2 k-1-h&lt;2 k-1+h</span>, there is only one possibility, namely</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{aligned}\n&amp; 2 k-1-h=2 \\text { and } \\\\\n&amp; 2 k-1+h=8 .\n\\end{aligned}</span></p><p>This system has solution <span class=\"tex\">(h, k)=(3,3)</span>. So <span class=\"tex\">M A=7</span> and <span class=\"tex\">M C=9</span>. Then <span class=\"tex\">a^{2}=7^{2}-3^{2}=40</span> and <span class=\"tex\">b^{2}=9^{2}-3^{2}=72</span>, so <span class=\"tex\">(a b)^{2}=40 \\cdot 72=8 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 8 \\cdot 9</span>, and hence <span class=\"tex\">a b=24 \\sqrt{5}</span>. The volume of the pyramid is therefore <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{1}{3} \\cdot h \\cdot a b=24 \\sqrt{5}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2021-spring/part-b/problem-14"}, {"q": "<p> The figure below is constructed from <span class=\"tex\">11</span> line segments, each of which has length <span class=\"tex\">2</span>. The area of pentagon <span class=\"tex\">A B C D E</span> can be written as <span class=\"tex\">\\sqrt{m}+\\sqrt{n}</span>, where <span class=\"tex\">m</span> and <span class=\"tex\">n</span> are positive integers. What is <span class=\"tex\">m+n?</span></p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc12/2021SpringB/2024-07-22_201656.png\" width=\"500\"/></div>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">20</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">21</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">22</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">23</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">24</span>"], "a": "D", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">20</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">21</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">22</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">23</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">24</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p><strong>Answer (D):</strong> Let <span class=\"tex\">M</span> be the midpoint of <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{C D}</span>, and label the remaining points <span class=\"tex\">P</span> and <span class=\"tex\">Q</span> as shown in the diagram below.</p>\n<div class=\"diagram\"><svg height=\"290px\" style=\"background: transparent; background-color: transparent; color-scheme: light dark;\" width=\"251px\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><defs></defs><g><g><g><g><g><image height=\"290\" width=\"250.77\"/></g></g></g></g></g></svg></div>\n<p>Observe that</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">[A B C D E]=[A B C]+[A C D]+[A D E],</span></p><p>where the brackets denote area. Because the four equilateral triangles have side length 2 , their areas are <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{2^{2} \\sqrt{3}}{4}= \\sqrt{3}</span> each. The sum <span class=\"tex\">[A B C]+[A D E]</span> is equal to the combined areas of two of these triangles and hence is equal to <span class=\"tex\">2 \\sqrt{3}</span>. To compute <span class=\"tex\">[A C D]</span>, observe that the triangle is isosceles with <span class=\"tex\">C D=2</span> and</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">A C=A D=2 \\operatorname{dist}(A, \\overline{B Q})=2 \\sqrt{3} .</span></p><p>Then <span class=\"tex\">A M=\\sqrt{A C^{2}-C M^{2}}=\\sqrt{11}</span>, so <span class=\"tex\">[A C D]=\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 2 \\cdot \\sqrt{11}=\\sqrt{11}</span>. Therefore</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">[A B C D E]=\\sqrt{11}+2 \\sqrt{3}=\\sqrt{11}+\\sqrt{12},</span></p><p>so <span class=\"tex\">m+n=11+12=23</span>.</p>\n<p><strong>OR</strong></p>\n<p>Because <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{A C}</span> has twice the length of the median of an equilateral triangle of side 2 , its length is <span class=\"tex\">2 \\sqrt{3}</span>, and the solution proceeds as above.</p>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong> This configuration is referred to as the <em>Moser spindle</em>. It is a unit-distance graph with chromatic number 4.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2021-spring/part-b/problem-15"}, {"q": "<p> Let <span class=\"tex\">g(x)</span> be a polynomial with leading coefficient <span class=\"tex\">1</span>, whose three roots are the reciprocals of the three roots of <span class=\"tex\">f(x)=x^{3}+a x^{2}+b x+c</span>, where <span class=\"tex\">1&lt;a&lt;b&lt;c</span>. What is <span class=\"tex\">g(1)</span> in terms of <span class=\"tex\">a, b</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">c?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1+a+b+c}{c}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">1+a+b+c</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1+a+b+c}{c^{2}}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{a+b+c}{c^{2}}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1+a+b+c}{a+b+c}</span>"], "a": "A", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1+a+b+c}{c}</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">1+a+b+c</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1+a+b+c}{c^{2}}</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{a+b+c}{c^{2}}</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1+a+b+c}{a+b+c}</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p><strong>Answer (A):</strong> Division of <span class=\"tex\">f(x)</span> by <span class=\"tex\">x^{3}</span> gives a cubic polynomial in <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{1}{x}</span>, whose roots are the roots of <span class=\"tex\">g</span>. Therefore</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">g(x)=\\frac{c x^{3}+b x^{2}+a x+1}{c},</span></p><p>so</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">g(1)=\\frac{c \\cdot 1^{3}+b \\cdot 1^{2}+a \\cdot 1+1}{c}=\\frac{1+a+b+c}{c} .</span></p><p><strong>OR</strong></p>\n<p>Suppose the roots of <span class=\"tex\">f</span> are <span class=\"tex\">r, s</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">t</span>. Then</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &amp; =(x-r)(x-s)(x-t) \\\\\n&amp; =x^{3}-(r+s+t) x^{2}+(r s+r t+s t) x-r s t,\n\\end{aligned}</span></p><p>so <span class=\"tex\">-a=r+s+t, b=r s+r t+s t</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">-c=r s t</span>. Because the roots of <span class=\"tex\">g</span> are <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{1}{r}, \\frac{1}{s}</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{1}{t}</span>,</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">g(x)=\\left(x-\\frac{1}{r}\\right)\\left(x-\\frac{1}{s}\\right)\\left(x-\\frac{1}{t}\\right)</span></p><p>and</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">g(1)=\\left(1-\\frac{1}{r}\\right)\\left(1-\\frac{1}{s}\\right)\\left(1-\\frac{1}{t}\\right) .</span></p><p>Expanding gives</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">g(1)=1-\\left(\\frac{1}{r}+\\frac{1}{s}+\\frac{1}{t}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{1}{r s}+\\frac{1}{r t}+\\frac{1}{s t}\\right)-\\frac{1}{r s t} .</span></p><p>Rewriting with common denominator <span class=\"tex\">r</span> st gives</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">g(1)=\\frac{r s t-(r s+r t+s t)+(r+s+t)-1}{r s t} .</span></p><p>Therefore</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">g(1)=\\frac{-c-b-a-1}{-c}=\\frac{1+a+b+c}{c} .</span></p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2021-spring/part-b/problem-16"}, {"q": "<p> Let <span class=\"tex\">A B C D</span> be an isoceles trapezoid having parallel bases <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{A B}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{C D}</span> with <span class=\"tex\">A B&gt;C D</span>. Line segments from a point inside <span class=\"tex\">A B C D</span> to the vertices divide the trapezoid into four triangles whose areas are <span class=\"tex\">2,3,4</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">5</span> starting with the triangle with base <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{C D}</span> and moving clockwise as shown in the diagram below. What is the ratio <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{A B}{C D}?</span></p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc12/2021SpringB/2023_11_14_f0ca9305ab054e45278bg-008.jpg\" width=\"500\"/></div>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">3</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">2+\\sqrt{2}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">1+\\sqrt{6}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">2 \\sqrt{3}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">3 \\sqrt{2}</span>"], "a": "B", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">3</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">2+\\sqrt{2}</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">1+\\sqrt{6}</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">2 \\sqrt{3}</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">3 \\sqrt{2}</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p><strong>Answer (B):</strong> Let <span class=\"tex\">X</span> and <span class=\"tex\">Y</span> be the feet of the perpendiculars from interior point <span class=\"tex\">P</span> to <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{A B}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{C D}</span>, respectively. Let <span class=\"tex\">r=A B</span> and <span class=\"tex\">s=C D</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"diagram\"><svg height=\"293px\" style=\"background: transparent; background-color: transparent; color-scheme: light dark;\" width=\"496px\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><defs></defs><g><g><g><g><g><image height=\"293\" width=\"496\"/></g></g></g></g></g></svg></div>\n<p>The area of <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle A P B=\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot r \\cdot P X=4</span>. Thus <span class=\"tex\">P X=\\frac{8}{r}</span>. Similarly, <span class=\"tex\">P Y=\\frac{4}{s}</span>. Now using the formula for the area of a trapezoid yields</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">14=\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot X Y \\cdot(A B+C D)=\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{8}{r}+\\frac{4}{s}\\right)(r+s)=6+4 \\cdot \\frac{s}{r}+2 \\cdot \\frac{r}{s}</span></p><p>Thus the ratio <span class=\"tex\">\\rho=\\frac{r}{s}</span> satisfies <span class=\"tex\">\\rho+2 \\rho^{-1}=4</span>; solving yields <span class=\"tex\">\\rho=2 \\pm \\sqrt{2}</span>. Because <span class=\"tex\">r&gt;s</span>, the ratio <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{r}{s}</span> is <span class=\"tex\">2+\\sqrt{2}</span>. Indeed, such a trapezoid exists. Let <span class=\"tex\">s=1</span> and <span class=\"tex\">r=2+\\sqrt{2}</span>. The height from base <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{A B}</span> to point <span class=\"tex\">P</span> is <span class=\"tex\">8-4 \\sqrt{2}</span>, and the height from base <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{C D}</span> to point <span class=\"tex\">P</span> is 4 . Thus the height of the trapezoid is <span class=\"tex\">12-4 \\sqrt{2}</span> and the area of the trapezoid is</p>\n<p>Also, <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle A B P</span> has area</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot(3+\\sqrt{2}) \\cdot(12-4 \\sqrt{2})=14</span></p><p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot(2+\\sqrt{2})(8-4 \\sqrt{2})=4</span></p><p>and <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle C D P</span> has area</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 1 \\cdot 4=2</span></p><p>Point <span class=\"tex\">P</span> is <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{5}{8}</span> of the way from left to right along the line segment extending across the interior of the trapezoid through <span class=\"tex\">P</span> parallel to the bases. Because the combined area of <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle A D P</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle C P B</span> is <span class=\"tex\">14-6=8</span>, this placement of <span class=\"tex\">P</span> will ensure that the areas of <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle A D P</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle C P B</span> are 5 and 3 , respectively, as needed.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2021-spring/part-b/problem-17"}, {"q": "<p> Let <span class=\"tex\">z</span> be a complex number satisfying</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">12|z|^{2}=2|z+2|^{2}+\\left|z^{2}+1\\right|^{2}+31</span></p><p>What is the value of <span class=\"tex\">z+\\dfrac{6}{z} ?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">-2</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">-1</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{2}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">1</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">4</span>"], "a": "A", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">-2</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">-1</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{2}</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">1</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">4</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p><strong>Answer (A):</strong> Recall that if <span class=\"tex\">w</span> is a complex number and <span class=\"tex\">\\bar{w}</span> denotes its conjugate, then <span class=\"tex\">w \\cdot \\bar{w}=|w|^{2}</span>. Using this relationship and properties of conjugation, the given equation can be rewritten as</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">12 z \\bar{z}=2(z+2)(\\bar{z}+2)+\\left(z^{2}+1\\right)\\left(\\bar{z}^{2}+1\\right)+31</span></p><p>This is equivalent to <span class=\"tex\">12 z \\bar{z}=2 z \\bar{z}+4 z+4 \\bar{z}+8+(z \\bar{z})^{2}+z^{2}+\\bar{z}^{2}+1+31</span>. Rearranging the terms gives</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\left((z \\bar{z})^{2}-12 z \\bar{z}+36\\right)+\\left(z^{2}+\\bar{z}^{2}+2 z \\bar{z}+4 z+4 \\bar{z}+4\\right)=0</span></p><p>This implies that <span class=\"tex\">(z \\bar{z}-6)^{2}+(z+\\bar{z}+2)^{2}=0</span>. Because <span class=\"tex\">z \\bar{z}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">z+\\bar{z}</span> are both real numbers, it must be that <span class=\"tex\">z \\bar{z}-6=0</span> and <span class=\"tex\">z+\\bar{z}+2=0</span>. Thus <span class=\"tex\">z \\bar{z}=6</span> and <span class=\"tex\">z+\\bar{z}=-2</span>, so <span class=\"tex\">z+\\frac{6}{z}=z+\\bar{z}=-2</span>.</p>\n<p>Note: The values of <span class=\"tex\">z</span> that satisfy the given equation are <span class=\"tex\">-1 \\pm i \\sqrt{5}</span>, for which each side equals 72 .</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2021-spring/part-b/problem-18"}, {"q": "<p> Two fair dice, each with at least <span class=\"tex\">6</span> faces, are rolled. On each face of each die is printed a distinct integer from <span class=\"tex\">1</span> to the number of faces on that die, inclusive. The probability of rolling a sum of <span class=\"tex\">7</span> is <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{3}{4}</span> of the probability of rolling a sum of <span class=\"tex\">10</span> and the probability of rolling a sum of <span class=\"tex\">12</span> is <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{12}</span>. What is the least possible number of faces on the two dice combined?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">16</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">17</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">18</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">19</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">20</span>"], "a": "B", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">16</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">17</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">18</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">19</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">20</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p><strong>Answer (B):</strong> Because each die has at least 6 faces, the number of ways to roll a total of 7 is 6 , namely with rolls <span class=\"tex\">(1,6),(2,5),(3,4),(4,3),(5,2)</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">(6,1)</span>. Because the probability of rolling a total of 7 is <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{3}{4}</span> that of rolling a total of 10 , the number of ways to roll a 10 is <span class=\"tex\">6 \\div \\frac{3}{4}=8</span>. There are 9 possible combinations of two positive integers that total 10 , and thus one of these combinations cannot be made with the numbers on the two dice. Therefore one of the combinations <span class=\"tex\">(1,9)</span> and <span class=\"tex\">(9,1)</span> may be rolled, but not the other, but both <span class=\"tex\">(8,2)</span> and <span class=\"tex\">(2,8)</span> are possible. This implies one die has exactly 8 faces and the other die has 9 or more faces. If the other die has 9 faces, there are 72 possible rolls, of which 6 add to 12 . Then the probability of rolling a total of 12 would be <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{6}{72}=\\frac{1}{12}</span>, as required. There are <span class=\"tex\">8+9=17</span> faces on the two dice combined. (Dice with 8 and 12 faces also have the stated property, because in that case the probability of rolling a total of 12 would be <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{8}{96}=\\frac{1}{12}</span>.)</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2021-spring/part-b/problem-19"}, {"q": "<p> Let <span class=\"tex\">Q(z)</span> and <span class=\"tex\">R(z)</span> be the unique polynomials such that</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">z^{2021}+1=\\left(z^{2}+z+1\\right) Q(z)+R(z)</span></p><p>and the degree of <span class=\"tex\">R</span> is less than <span class=\"tex\">2</span>. What is <span class=\"tex\">R(z)?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">-z</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">-1</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">2021</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">z+1</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">2 z+1</span>"], "a": "A", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">-z</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">-1</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">2021</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">z+1</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">2 z+1</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p><strong>Answer (A):</strong> Note that</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">z^{n+3}+1-\\left(z^{n}+1\\right)=z^{n}\\left(z^{3}-1\\right)=z^{n}(z-1)\\left(z^{2}+z+1\\right) .</span></p><p>Therefore <span class=\"tex\">z^{n+3}+1</span> and <span class=\"tex\">z^{n}+1</span> leave the same remainder when divided by <span class=\"tex\">z^{2}+z+1</span>. Because <span class=\"tex\">2021 \\equiv 2(\\bmod 3)</span>, the remainder when <span class=\"tex\">z^{2021}+1</span> is divided by <span class=\"tex\">z^{2}+z+1</span> is the same as the remainder when <span class=\"tex\">z^{2}+1</span> is divided by <span class=\"tex\">z^{2}+z+1</span>, namely <span class=\"tex\">-z</span>.</p>\n<p><strong>OR</strong></p>\n<p>The solutions of <span class=\"tex\">z^{2}+z+1=0</span> are <span class=\"tex\">z=\\frac{-1 \\pm i \\sqrt{3}}{2}</span>. Therefore</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{aligned}\nR\\left(\\frac{-1+i \\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) &amp; =R\\left(\\operatorname{cis} \\frac{2 \\pi}{3}\\right) \\\\\n&amp; =\\left(\\operatorname{cis} \\frac{2 \\pi}{3}\\right)^{2021}+1 \\\\\n&amp; =\\left(\\operatorname{cis} \\frac{4042 \\pi}{3}\\right)+1 \\\\\n&amp; =\\left(\\operatorname{cis} \\frac{4 \\pi}{3}\\right)+1 \\\\\n&amp; =\\frac{1-i \\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}</span></p><p>and</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{aligned}\nR\\left(\\frac{-1-i \\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) &amp; =R\\left(\\operatorname{cis} \\frac{4 \\pi}{3}\\right) \\\\\n&amp; =\\left(\\operatorname{cis} \\frac{4 \\pi}{3}\\right)^{2021}+1 \\\\\n&amp; =\\left(\\operatorname{cis} \\frac{8084 \\pi}{3}\\right)+1 \\\\\n&amp; =\\left(\\operatorname{cis} \\frac{2 \\pi}{3}\\right)+1 \\\\\n&amp; =\\frac{1+i \\sqrt{3}}{2} .\n\\end{aligned}</span></p><p>Because <span class=\"tex\">R(z)</span> is linear and <span class=\"tex\">R(z)=-z</span> for each of the solutions of <span class=\"tex\">z^{2}+z+1=0</span>, it follows that <span class=\"tex\">R(z)=-z</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2021-spring/part-b/problem-20"}, {"q": "<p> Let <span class=\"tex\">S</span> be the sum of all positive real numbers <span class=\"tex\">x</span> for which</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">x^{2^{\\sqrt{2}}}=\\sqrt{2}^{2^{x}}</span></p><p>Which of the following statements is true?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">S&lt;\\sqrt{2}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">S=\\sqrt{2}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\sqrt{2}&lt;S&lt;2</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">2 \\leq S&lt;6</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">S \\geq 6</span>"], "a": "D", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">S&lt;\\sqrt{2}</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">S=\\sqrt{2}</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">\\sqrt{2}&lt;S&lt;2</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">2 \\leq S&lt;6</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">S \\geq 6</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p><strong>Answer (D):</strong> Taking the logarithm to base <span class=\"tex\">\\sqrt{2}</span> of each side of the equation gives</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">2^{\\sqrt{2}} \\log _{\\sqrt{2}} x=2^{x} .</span></p><p>This is equivalent to <span class=\"tex\">\\log _{\\sqrt{2}} x=2^{x-\\sqrt{2}}</span>. The shapes of the graphs of the two functions in this latter equation reveal where they intersect. The logarithmic function is increasing and concave down, while the exponential function is increasing and concave up. Hence their graphs can intersect in at most two points. One straightforward solution is <span class=\"tex\">x=\\sqrt{2}</span>. To see that there is in fact another solution, observe that <span class=\"tex\">\\log _{\\sqrt{2}} 2=2&gt;2^{2-\\sqrt{2}}</span>, whereas <span class=\"tex\">\\log _{\\sqrt{2}} 4=4&lt;2^{4-\\sqrt{2}}</span>. Therefore a solution must exist between 2 and 4 . It follows that the sum of the solutions must be in the interval <span class=\"tex\">(2+\\sqrt{2}, 4+\\sqrt{2})</span>. Thus <span class=\"tex\">2 \\leq S&lt;6</span>. See the graphs below.</p>\n<div class=\"diagram\"><svg height=\"350px\" style=\"background: transparent; background-color: transparent; color-scheme: light dark;\" width=\"441px\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><defs></defs><g><g><g><g><g><image height=\"350\" width=\"440.38\"/></g></g></g></g></g></svg></div>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2021-spring/part-b/problem-21"}, {"q": "<p> Arjun and Beth play a game in which they take turns removing one brick or two adjacent bricks from one \"wall\" among a set of several walls of bricks, with gaps possibly creating new walls. The walls are one brick tall. For example, a set of walls of sizes <span class=\"tex\">4</span> and <span class=\"tex\">2</span> can be changed into any of the following by one move: <span class=\"tex\">(3,2),(2,1,2),(4),(4,1),(2,2)</span>, or <span class=\"tex\">(1,1,2)</span>. Arjun plays first, and the player who removes the last brick wins. For which starting configuration is there a strategy that guarantees a win for Beth?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">(6,1,1)</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">(6,2,1)</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">(6,2,2)</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">(6,3,1)</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">(6,3,2)</span>"], "a": "B", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">(6,1,1)</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">(6,2,1)</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">(6,2,2)</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">(6,3,1)</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">(6,3,2)</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p><strong>Answer (B):</strong> Observe that a player who can leave two (or any even number of) copies of each wall size can win by mirroring the opponent's moves in the other copy. For any configuration except (B), Arjun has a move that leaves two or four copies of each wall size. Specifically these moves include generating walls of sizes <span class=\"tex\">(2,2,1,1),(2,2,2,2),(3,1,3,1)</span>, and (3, 2, 3, 2 ) from choices (A), (C), (D), and (E), respectively. Only choice (B) gives Beth a chance.</p>\n<p>It remains to show that Beth can win from configuration (B). With two exceptions, any starting move Arjun can make allows Beth to create a position from which she wins by the mirroring strategy. The exceptions are when Arjun takes away the wall of size 1 or the wall of size 2 . In either case, Beth must take from the wall of size 6, leaving ( <span class=\"tex\">1,3,2</span> ) or ( <span class=\"tex\">2,3,1</span> ), respectively. Then any move by Arjun leaves Beth able to create a mirroring position and win.</p>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong> This combinatorial game is called Kayles.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2021-spring/part-b/problem-22"}, {"q": "<p> Three balls are randomly and independently tossed into bins numbered with the positive integers so that for each ball, the probability it is tossed into bin <span class=\"tex\">i</span> is <span class=\"tex\">2^{-i}</span> for <span class=\"tex\">i=1,2,3, \\ldots</span>. More than one ball is allowed in each bin. The probability that the balls end up evenly spaced in distinct bins is <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{p}{q}</span>, where <span class=\"tex\">p</span> and <span class=\"tex\">q</span> are relatively prime positive integers. (For example, the balls are evenly spaced if they are tossed into bins <span class=\"tex\">3,17</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">10</span>.) What is <span class=\"tex\">p+q?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">55</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">56</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">57</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">58</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">59</span>"], "a": "A", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">55</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">56</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">57</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">58</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">59</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p><strong>Answer (A):</strong> First compute the probability that the bin numbers in which the balls land, in the order they are tossed, form an increasing arithmetic sequence; the final answer will be 6 times this number to account for all possible orders. The probability that the bin numbers satisfy this requirement with the first ball getting tossed into bin 1 is</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2^{1}}\\left(\\frac{1}{2^{2}} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2^{3}}+\\frac{1}{2^{3}} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2^{5}}+\\frac{1}{2^{4}} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2^{7}}+\\cdots\\right) &amp; =\\frac{1}{2} \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{2^{n+1}} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2^{2 n+1}} \\\\\n&amp; =\\frac{1}{2} \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{2^{3 n+2}} \\\\\n&amp; =\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2^{5}} \\cdot \\frac{1}{1-\\frac{1}{8}} \\\\\n&amp; =\\frac{1}{56}\n\\end{aligned}</span></p><p>Similarly, if the first ball is tossed into bin <span class=\"tex\">i</span>, then the probability that the bin numbers satisfy this requirement is</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\frac{1}{2^{i}} \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{2^{3 n+2 i}}=\\frac{1}{7} \\cdot \\frac{1}{8^{i}} .</span></p><p>Summing over all values of <span class=\"tex\">i</span> gives</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\sum_{i=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{7} \\cdot \\frac{1}{8^{i}}=\\frac{1}{7} \\cdot \\frac{\\frac{1}{8}}{1-\\frac{1}{8}}=\\frac{1}{49}</span></p><p>Multiplying by 6 gives the required probability as <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{6}{49}</span>, so the requested sum is <span class=\"tex\">6+49=55</span>.</p>\n<p><strong>OR</strong></p>\n<p>Let <span class=\"tex\">E_{i, d}</span> denote the event that the first ball falls in bin <span class=\"tex\">i</span>, the second ball falls in bin <span class=\"tex\">i+d</span>, and the third ball falls in bin <span class=\"tex\">i+2 d</span>, and note that <span class=\"tex\">\\mathbf{P}\\left(E_{i, d}\\right)=2^{-3 i-3 d}</span>. Let <span class=\"tex\">E</span> be the union of the disjoint events <span class=\"tex\">E_{i, d}</span>, then</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\mathbf{P}(E)=\\sum_{i=1}^{\\infty} \\sum_{d=1}^{\\infty} \\mathbf{P}\\left(E_{i, d}\\right)=\\left(\\sum_{i=1}^{\\infty} 2^{-3 i}\\right)\\left(\\sum_{d=1}^{\\infty} 2^{-3 d}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{\\frac{1}{8}}{1-\\frac{1}{8}}\\right)^{2}=\\frac{1}{49} .</span></p><p>The balls may fall in bins <span class=\"tex\">i, i+d, i+2 d</span> in any of <span class=\"tex\">3!=6</span> orders, so the desired probability is <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{6}{49}</span> and the requested sum is <span class=\"tex\">6+49=55</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2021-spring/part-b/problem-23"}, {"q": "<p> Let <span class=\"tex\">A B C D</span> be a parallelogram with area <span class=\"tex\">15</span>. Points <span class=\"tex\">P</span> and <span class=\"tex\">Q</span> are the projections of <span class=\"tex\">A</span> and <span class=\"tex\">C</span>, respectively, onto the line <span class=\"tex\">B D</span>; and points <span class=\"tex\">R</span> and <span class=\"tex\">S</span> are the projections of <span class=\"tex\">B</span> and <span class=\"tex\">D</span>, respectively, onto the line <span class=\"tex\">A C</span>. See the figure, which also shows the relative locations of these points.</p>\n<div class=\"diagram\"><svg height=\"190px\" style=\"background: transparent; background-color: transparent; color-scheme: light dark;\" width=\"421px\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><defs></defs><g><g><g><g><g><image height=\"190\" width=\"420.28\"/></g></g></g></g></g></svg></div>\n<p>Suppose <span class=\"tex\">P Q=6</span> and <span class=\"tex\">R S=8</span>, and let <span class=\"tex\">d</span> denote the length of <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{B D}</span>, the longer diagonal of <span class=\"tex\">A B C D</span>. Then <span class=\"tex\">d^{2}</span> can be written in the form <span class=\"tex\">m+n \\sqrt{p}</span>, where <span class=\"tex\">m, n</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">p</span> are positive integers and <span class=\"tex\">p</span> is not divisible by the square of any prime. What is <span class=\"tex\">m+n+p?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">81</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">89</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">97</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">105</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">113</span>"], "a": "A", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">81</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">89</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">97</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">105</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">113</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p><strong>Answer (A):</strong> Let <span class=\"tex\">X</span> denote the intersection point of the diagonals <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{A C}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{B D}</span>. Note that by symmetry <span class=\"tex\">X</span> is the midpoint of both <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{P Q}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{R S}</span>, so <span class=\"tex\">X P=X Q=3</span> and <span class=\"tex\">X R=X S=4</span>. Because <span class=\"tex\">\\angle A P B=\\angle A R B=90^{\\circ}</span>, quadrilateral <span class=\"tex\">A B R P</span> is cyclic, and the power of the point <span class=\"tex\">X</span> is <span class=\"tex\">X R \\cdot X A=X P \\cdot X B</span>. Therefore</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\frac{X A}{X B}=\\frac{X P}{X R}=\\frac{3}{4} .</span></p><p>Thus there is an <span class=\"tex\">x&gt;0</span> such that <span class=\"tex\">X A=3 x</span> and <span class=\"tex\">X B=4 x</span>. Applying the Pythagorean Theorem to <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle A P X</span> yields <span class=\"tex\">A P=\\sqrt{A X^{2}-X P^{2}}=3 \\sqrt{x^{2}-1}</span>, so</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">[A B C D]=2[A B D]=A P \\cdot B D=3 \\sqrt{x^{2}-1} \\cdot 8 x=24 x \\sqrt{x^{2}-1}=15 .</span></p><p>Solving this for <span class=\"tex\">x^{2}</span> yields <span class=\"tex\">x^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{8}</span>, so</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">d^{2}=(8 x)^{2}=64 x^{2}=64\\left(\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{8}\\right)=32+8 \\sqrt{41} .</span></p><p>The requested sum is <span class=\"tex\">32+8+41=81</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"diagram\"><svg height=\"280px\" style=\"background: transparent; background-color: transparent; color-scheme: light dark;\" width=\"369px\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><defs></defs><g><g><g><g><g><image height=\"280\" width=\"368.53\"/></g></g></g></g></g></svg></div>\n<p><strong>OR</strong></p>\n<p>Let <span class=\"tex\">X</span> denote the intersection of <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{A C}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{B D}</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">s=\\sin \\angle A X B</span>. Note that <span class=\"tex\">\\angle A X B</span> is the external angle of right triangles <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle A P X</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle B R X</span>, implying that <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle A P X \\sim \\triangle B R X</span> with ratio <span class=\"tex\">3: 4</span>. Then there is a real number <span class=\"tex\">z</span> such that <span class=\"tex\">A C=3 z</span> and <span class=\"tex\">B D=4 z</span>. The area of <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle A X B</span> is</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\frac{1}{4} \\cdot 15=\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot A X \\cdot B X \\cdot s=\\frac{3}{2} \\cdot s z^{2} .</span></p><p>Hence</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">s=\\frac{5}{2 z^{2}}</span></p><p>Also, the Pythagorean Theorem applied to <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle B R X</span> gives <span class=\"tex\">B R=\\sqrt{4 z^{2}-16}</span>, so</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">s=\\frac{B R}{B X}=\\frac{\\sqrt{4 z^{2}-16}}{2 z}</span></p><p>Equating the two expressions for <span class=\"tex\">s</span> gives</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\frac{5}{2 z^{2}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{4 z^{2}-16}}{2 z}</span></p><p>which simplifies to <span class=\"tex\">4 z^{4}-16 z^{2}-25=0</span>. The solution <span class=\"tex\">B D^{2}=16 z^{2}=32+8 \\sqrt{41}</span> then follows.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2021-spring/part-b/problem-24"}, {"q": "<p> Let <span class=\"tex\">S</span> be the set of lattice points in the coordinate plane, both of whose coordinates are integers between <span class=\"tex\">1</span> and <span class=\"tex\">30</span>, inclusive. Exactly <span class=\"tex\">300</span> points in <span class=\"tex\">S</span> lie on or below a line with equation <span class=\"tex\">y=m x</span>. The possible values of <span class=\"tex\">m</span> lie in an interval of length <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{a}{b}</span>, where <span class=\"tex\">a</span> and <span class=\"tex\">b</span> are relatively prime positive integers. What is <span class=\"tex\">a+b?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">31</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">47</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">62</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">72</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">85</span>"], "a": "E", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">31</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">47</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">62</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">72</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">85</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p><strong>Answer (E):</strong> Because the triangle bounded by the <span class=\"tex\">x</span>-axis and the lines <span class=\"tex\">x=30</span> and <span class=\"tex\">y=\\frac{2}{3} x</span> has area <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 30 \\cdot 20=300</span>, it is reasonable to guess that the acceptable values of <span class=\"tex\">m</span> are near <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{2}{3}</span>. To count the number of elements of <span class=\"tex\">S</span> that are on or below the line <span class=\"tex\">y=\\frac{2}{3} x</span>, let <span class=\"tex\">s_{k}</span> be the number of such points with <span class=\"tex\">x=k</span> for <span class=\"tex\">1 \\leq k \\leq 30</span>. Note that <span class=\"tex\">s_{1}=0, s_{2}=1, s_{3}=2</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">s_{k+3}=s_{k}+2</span> for <span class=\"tex\">0 \\leq k \\leq 27</span>. Thus the number of elements of <span class=\"tex\">S</span> that are on or below the line is <span class=\"tex\">(0+1+2)+(2+3+4)+\\cdots+(18+19+20)=3(1+3+5+\\cdots+19)=300</span>. Because some lattice points in <span class=\"tex\">S</span> do lie on the line, <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{2}{3}</span> is the least possible value of <span class=\"tex\">m</span>.</p>\n<p>For <span class=\"tex\">1 \\leq k \\leq 10</span>, the line <span class=\"tex\">y=\\frac{2}{3} x</span> is <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{1}{3}</span> of a unit below the point <span class=\"tex\">(3 k-2,2 k-1), \\frac{2}{3}</span> of a unit below the point <span class=\"tex\">(3 k-1,2 k)</span>, and 1 unit below the point <span class=\"tex\">(3 k, 2 k+1)</span>. Therefore as <span class=\"tex\">m</span> increases from <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{2}{3}</span>, the first lattice point to be intersected by the line <span class=\"tex\">y=m x</span> is <span class=\"tex\">(3 \\cdot 10-2,2 \\cdot 10-1)=(28,19)</span>. Hence the possible values of <span class=\"tex\">m</span> satisfy <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{2}{3} \\leq m&lt;\\frac{19}{28}</span>, so they lie in an interval of length <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{19}{28}-\\frac{2}{3}=\\frac{1}{84}</span>. The requested sum is <span class=\"tex\">1+84=85</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2021-spring/part-b/problem-25"}]});
